{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790527884,"nonce":"UlwnnLWEQul7IEhC8PmtJCs/aMw=","title":"wasm AOT: reload a module from a newly compiled side module","message":"Requested by caribou's dev server (caribou git-bug 47efa60d7bff0cbe4f71a72299616fd8ac3de96bf19616a9e800510bd8dfedfa). An AOT wasm program has no interpreter or JIT, so vm.reload_module (re-run from source) cannot apply. Instead the dev server compiles the changed module into a side module with the AOT object path, and Ash's page host loads it into the running program. WrenLift has to reload the module from that code: the module's entry and its classes' method bodies. Existing classes keep their identity, so objects made before the reload get the new bodies, and the module body re-runs, as the source reload does. Caribou then republishes the interface and bumps its call-site epoch.\n\nA possible shape is wlift_aot_reload_module(vm, name, entry). The side module's registration hands the runtime its entry, and the runtime installs it against the loaded module. What stands in the way today:\n\n- Class identity. wlift_aot_install_class always allocates a new ObjClass. A reload has to find the module's existing class by name and replace its method table in place, as vm.reload_module does through `preserved`. It must skip core classes and flush the method cache and inline caches.\n- Module variables. Each module's variables are a data blob owned by the program's bootstrap, and imports copy slot values from one blob to another at startup. A separately compiled side module gets a blob of its own. The reloaded code has to use the running module's blob, either passed in by the runtime or reached through one indirection, so top-level state and importers stay coherent. The runtime has no map from module name to blob today; the bootstrap knows it only statically.\n- Registration. A library's global constructor calls wlift_aot_register_program, and wlift_aot_run_programs runs every registered program once. A reloadable module needs its own entry that registers against a module name and does not re-run on the next run_programs.\n- Direct calls and inlining. LLVM AOT calls a known implementation's compiled body directly, behind a class check (aot_direct_call). #export symbols do the same, constructors are allocated and initialised inline, and the whole program is one LLVM module, so bodies can inline across modules. Code outside the reloaded module would keep running the old bodies. A dev build needs a mode where calls into reloadable modules go through dispatch (class.methods), with no direct calls, CHA or cross-module inlining.\n- Layout. A reload that adds fields leaves existing instances too small; 9ab7c22 is the same problem for the source reload.\n- Imports from the reloaded module into the running program resolve through wlift_aot_resolve_runtime_import, and the side module's own constants and symbols are interned at load. Both should work unchanged; check them.","files":null}]}